# MAIA-300 VRAM Residency Model

This repository documents a VRAM residency model for the MAIA-300 GPU architecture.

The goal is to estimate realistic allocator-stable VRAM residency under sustained compute workloads and memory allocator behavior.

The model derives stable residency levels from total HBM4 capacity and applies distribution across memory stacks and channels.

---

## Memory Capacity

Total HBM4 VRAM:

288 GB

---

## Stable Residency Estimate

Observed stable residency:

~212 GB

Residency ratio:

212 / 288 ≈ 0.736

≈ 73.6% stable VRAM utilization

---

## Residency Breakdown

288 GB total VRAM
↓
~268 GB usable memory (driver/system overhead removed)
↓
~241 GB allocator-effective memory
↓
~212 GB stable residency
↓
~73.6% sustained utilization

---

## HBM Distribution

MAIA-300 HBM architecture assumed:

8 HBM stacks  
16 memory channels per stack

Stable distribution:

Per HBM stack:

212 GB / 8 ≈ 26.5 GB

Per memory channel:

26.5 GB / 16 ≈ 1.66 GB

---

## Resulting Working Set Model

Total VRAM: 288 GB

Stable residency: ~212 GB

Per stack working set: ~26.5 GB

Per channel working set: ~1.66 GB

---

## Engineering Interpretation

The model represents the expected stable allocator residency plateau for large-scale compute workloads when memory allocation overhead, driver reservation, and fragmentation effects are accounted for.

This produces a stable VRAM operating window rather than theoretical full capacity.

---

## Validation Context

The residency model is derived from allocator-stable GPU operation and NVML telemetry observations under sustained workload conditions.

Observed characteristics:

• stable VRAM plateau  
• reduced allocation oscillation  
• predictable residency envelope

